WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.642
NOT IN looks like a simple anti-membership test.

00:00:03.642 --> 00:00:11.152
But if the inner set contains NULL, PostgreSQL cannot prove that an outer value differs from the unknown

00:00:11.152 --> 00:00:14.837
value. The condition can become UNKNOWN rather than TRUE.

00:00:14.837 --> 00:00:20.012
Because WHERE keeps only TRUE, rows you expected may disappear.

00:00:20.012 --> 00:00:23.937
This is one of the most important edge cases in this module.

00:00:23.937 --> 00:00:30.682
IN answers a membership question: is this value present in the set returned by the subquery?

00:00:30.682 --> 00:00:35.892
Be especially careful with NOT IN when NULL can appear in that set,

00:00:35.892 --> 00:00:40.792
because three-valued logic can turn expected TRUE results into UNKNOWN.

00:00:40.792 --> 00:00:45.715
For anti-matching, NOT EXISTS is often easier to reason about.
